lysis (EDA) concepts. We'll start from the very basics, and make it just a teeny lil bit more complex with every next code cell.\n","\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"ITOnbgXJ0il5"},"source":["# Python"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"QghnYypswwn4"},"source":["Python is a simple to use, [*very-high-level language*](https://www.javatpoint.com/classification-of-programming-languages). This means that it is easy for Programmers to understand Python compared to the machine.\n","\n","So, no worrying about semi-colons, wohooooo!! 🎉\n","But we now worry about indentation :/\n","\n","\n","It is very easy to compile, esp on Colab, giving you a lot of structure and support for large programs, and it is by far the most popular language to learn ML.\n","\n","\n","You will really start to appreciate the many many libraries you can import and just use directly once you get some practice. You don't get all this in C/C++/Java 😅\n","\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["\n","Python has extensive official documentation, and it’s always a good habit to get used to reading it early on.\n","\n","Getting into the habit of reading documentation early on is always beneficial. It not only helps you understand tools and libraries better, but also makes you more independent when solving problems. If you start practicing this now, it will be especially useful during your RP in the future.\n","\n"],"metadata":{"id":"51MyBiBfk1iD"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"z-rcbXjiFLBb"},"source":["Let's start🕺💃!"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"hn9TKga_0KKa"},"source":["##printing & data-types"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"xFY12Iny6_4C"},"source":["As is tradition, first we write a basic \"Hello, World!\" in Python."]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Put your text in the double quotes given below.\n","print(\"Hello, World!\")\n","# Now, to experiment.\n","# Does this work if you put the string in single quotes?\n","print('Hellow, World!')\n","# How about '''Like THIS'''?\n","print('''Hello, World! ''')"],"metadata":{"id":"HBgOBil7sEjr","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1758542349412,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":34,"user":{"displayName":"Risha Deshmukh","userId":"01255831594076528305"}},"outputId":"151a6ef4-482b-428b-f444-95a495e4d6fa"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Hello, World!\n","Hellow, World!\n","Hello, World! \n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"82FeBMScGAon"},"source":["When this ran, notice that all three \"Hello, World!\"s are on different lines.\n","\n","(Remember println/print in Java?)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["You need to now assign the Strings:\n","\n","\"Synapse is the best!\",\n","\n","\"AI stand for Actually Indians\" and p.s IYKYK🫠\n","\n","\"Why this Kolaveri Kolaveri Di\"\n","\n","to variables, and print them on the same line, using the function print()"],"metadata":{"id":"4Bog-jDPsUGG"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["a= \"Synapse is the best!\"\n","b= \"AI stand for Actually Indians\"\n","c= \"Why this Kolaveri Kolaveri Di\"\n","\n","\n","# First, only use one print() function call, without concatenating the Strings.\n","print(a,b,c)\n","\n","# Now, try concatenating the strings.\n","print(a + \" \"+ b+ \" \"+ c)\n","# Notice where you got spaces readymade, between sentences?\n","# Now you must use a different print() call for each string\n","# The output must still be on the same line!!\n","print(a, end=\" \")\n","print(b, end=\" \")\n","print(c)\n","\n","# Finally, use an f-string to create a single, formatted sentence.\n","# The output should look exactly like this:\n","# My affirmations are: \"Synapse is the best!\" , \"AI stand for Actually Indians\" and \"Why this Kolaveri Kolaveri Di\"\n","print(f\"My affirmations are : \\\"{a}\\\", \\\"{b}\\\" and \\\"{c}\\\"\")"],"metadata":{"id":"-5dGNwkStJqc","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1758542898621,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":27,"user":{"displayName":"Risha Deshmukh","userId":"01255831594076528305"}},"outputId":"8bd00296-3f25-403d-fc6a-90f365f896b4"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Synapse is the best! AI stand for Actually Indians Why this Kolaveri Kolaveri Di\n","Synapse is the best! AI stand for Actually Indians Why this Kolaveri Kolaveri Di\n","Synapse is the best! AI stand for Actually Indians Why this Kolaveri Kolaveri Di\n","My affirmations are : \"Synapse is the best!\", \"AI stand for Actually Indians\" and \"Why this Kolaveri Kolaveri Di\"\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Also, look up r-strings in python and gimme an example of it\n","\n","Answer:"],"metadata":{"id":"Fo29zGSMuU9p"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"QpIBhP1g6tT8"},"source":["While most production / project code does not take a direct input, ever, the input() function is your cin/scanf/scanner input method for Python.\n","\n","It returns the user entered inputs as a **String**, and prints the message you give it as an argument while asking for the input."]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Ask for a name, and assign it to a variable.\n","name = input(\"Enter your name \")\n","# say Hi to the name you got.\n","print(\"Hi\",name )\n","\n","# You do need integer/other inputs too, sometimes! Strings won't always do.\n","\n","\n","# Take an integer input, using the input() function and converting it to int\n","num = int(input(\"Enter number \"))\n","# Now, print the integer and its square, to see that you can use the integer\n","print(num, pow(num,2))"],"metadata":{"id":"VLJeTCdkuiE5","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1758543131261,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":6550,"user":{"displayName":"Risha Deshmukh","userId":"01255831594076528305"}},"outputId":"c0158c62-1d12-47c5-a690-88e1bfb2d635"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Enter your name Risha\n","Hi Risha\n","Enter number 5\n","5 25\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"MHPLlvUEzA2F"},"source":["**Lists**!! Everyone's first Data Structure.\n","\n","Lists in Python are similar to Arrays in your other languages, but much easier, because you do not have to give a fixed size, and can append & remove any elements.\n","\n","You can access elements using indices, starting from 0, all the way to length_of_list - 1\n","\n","\n","Question timeee : In languages like C++ and Java, primitive data types such as int or float have fixed upper and lower limits. Does Python also impose such caps on its numeric data types? If not, how does Python handle very large numbers?\n","\n","Answer:"]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Lists, in Python, can have elements of multiple datatypes together.\n","# No need for a list to be only of Strings/Integers etc.\n","\n","this_list_is_valid = [\"Synapse\", 2025 , (1,2,3), {\"andrew\":\"ng\",1:2}]\n","\n","this_list_is_valid[1] = 2025\n","\n","this_list_is_valid[1]\n","# If you end a codeblock with a variable like this,\n","# Python will print the value for you. Neat!"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"6GDL_A9uv1dF","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1758544523236,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":34,"user":{"displayName":"Risha Deshmukh","userId":"01255831594076528305"}},"outputId":"55cc6b2f-805d-4cd7-d48c-42c0519e8e8c"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":["2025"]},"metadata":{},"execution_count":17}]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Create a list of 5 elements\n","# and find + use as many Python methods on it as possible.\n","\n","# At least 10 different methods required. Make sure to use map,filter and reduce\n","my_list = [\"Synapse\", \"ML\", 1111, (12,13,14), {\"learning\":\"Python\",3:4}]\n","\n","# and your many operations from here!\n","from functools import reduce\n","\n","print(\"Original list:\", my_list)\n","\n","my_list.append(\"AI\")\n","print(\"After append:\", my_list)\n","\n","my_list.insert(2, \"Data Science\")\n","print(\"After insert:\", my_list)\n","\n","my_list.remove(\"ML\")\n","print(\"After remove:\", my_list)\n","\n","popped = my_list.pop()\n","print(\"Popped element:\", popped)\n","print(\"After pop:\", my_list)\n","\n","idx = my_list.index(\"Synapse\")\n","print(\"Index of 'Synapse':\", idx)\n","\n","cnt = my_list.count(\"Synapse\")\n","print(\"Count of 'Synapse':\", cnt)\n","\n","my_list.reverse()\n","print(\"After reverse:\", my_list)\n","\n","copy_list = my_list.copy()\n","print(\"Copy of list:\", copy_list)\n","\n","my_list.extend([99, 100])\n","print(\"After extend:\", my_list)\n","\n","copy_list.clear()\n","print(\"Cleared copy:\", copy_list)\n","\n","nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n","\n","squares = list(map(lambda x: x**2, nums))\n","print(\"Squares (map):\", squares)\n","\n","evens = list(filter(lambda x: x % 2 == 0, nums))\n","print(\"Evens (filter):\", evens)\n","\n","product = reduce(lambda x, y: x*y, nums)\n","print(\"Product (reduce):\", product)\n"],"metadata":{"id":"XePEmRY02dM6","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1758544525066,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":32,"user":{"displayName":"Risha Deshmukh","userId":"01255831594076528305"}},"outputId":"922ce1ad-d16f-4a85-b26e-8d25279d9444"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Original list: ['Synapse', 'ML', 1111, (12, 13, 14), {'learning': 'Python', 3: 4}]\n","After append: ['Synapse', 'ML', 1111, (12, 13, 14), {'learning': 'Python', 3: 4}, 'AI']\n","After insert: ['Synapse', 'ML', 'Data Science', 1111, (12, 13, 14), {'learning': 'Python', 3: 4}, 'AI']\n","After remove: ['Synapse', 'Data Science', 1111, (12, 13, 14), {'learning': 'Python', 3: 4}, 'AI']\n","Popped element: AI\n","After pop: ['Synapse', 'Data Science', 1111, (12, 13, 14), {'learning': 'Python', 3: 4}]\n","Index of 'Synapse': 0\n","Count of 'Synapse': 1\n","After reverse: [{'learning': 'Python', 3: 4}, (12, 13, 14), 1111, 'Data Science', 'Synapse']\n","Copy of list: [{'learning': 'Python', 3: 4}, (12, 13, 14), 1111, 'Data Science', 'Synapse']\n","After extend: [{'learning': 'Python', 3: 4}, (12, 13, 14), 1111, 'Data Science', 'Synapse', 99, 100]\n","Cleared copy: []\n","Squares (map): [1, 4, 9, 16, 25]\n","Evens (filter): [2, 4]\n","Product (reduce): 120\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["What is a lambda function?\n","\n","Answer: A lambda in Python is just a quick, one-line, nameless function. You use it when you need a small function for a short task and don’t want to write a full def.\n","\n","\n","What's the largest possible value that I can store in a python variable??\n","\n","largest = _No fixed maximum for integers – limited only by available memory,For floats, the largest is about 1.8e308___   (enter the value here)"],"metadata":{"id":"qAuvhhrF2sTb"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Tuples are exactly like lists, in every way except mutability.\n","\n","You access elements using indices, same as lists, but you cannot add/remove/modify them.\n","\n","They are useful in situations where you just need to declare something, usually critical, once. It could be used many times, and each element is accessible, but it cannot be changed."],"metadata":{"id":"OG8bJNzj3M_f"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# So what CAN you do with a Tuple?\n","# You tell us! Use the tuple you have.\n","\n","my_tuple = (10, 20, 30, 20, 40)\n","\n","print(\"First element:\", my_tuple[0])\n","print(\"Slice my_tuple[1:4]:\", my_tuple[1:4])\n","print(\"Count of 20:\", my_tuple.count(20))\n","print(\"Index of 30:\", my_tuple.index(30))\n","print(\"Length:\", len(my_tuple))\n","\n","print(\"Elements in tuple:\")\n","for item in my_tuple:\n","    print(item)\n","\n","a, b, c, d, e = my_tuple\n","print(\"Unpacked:\", a, b, c, d, e)\n","\n","nested = (1, (2, 3), (4, 5))\n","print(\"Nested tuple:\", nested)\n","\n","dictt = {(1, 2): \"point A\", (3, 4): \"point B\"}\n","print(\"Dictionary with tuple keys:\", dictt)\n"],"metadata":{"id":"YFXkEfJw3fac","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1758544562566,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":26,"user":{"displayName":"Risha Deshmukh","userId":"01255831594076528305"}},"outputId":"fed3e032-a9f3-4c73-9229-47261fab6681"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["First element: 10\n","Slice my_tuple[1:4]: (20, 30, 20)\n","Count of 20: 2\n","Index of 30: 2\n","Length: 5\n","Elements in tuple:\n","10\n","20\n","30\n","20\n","40\n","Unpacked: 10 20 30 20 40\n","Nested tuple: (1, (2, 3), (4, 5))\n","Dictionary with tuple keys: {(1, 2): 'point A', (3, 4): 'point B'}\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"CvVRJKiMNDxl"},"source":["**Dictionary** is interesting. You get to define your own indices!!\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Create a dicitionary and print it\n","my_dict ={\"Synapse\":\"Number 1 comittee\", \"ML\":\"Python\", \"College\":1}\n","print(my_dict)\n","\n","# update the value of an existing key\n","my_dict[\"ML\"] = \"Machine Learning\"\n","print(\"After updating ML:\", my_dict)\n","\n","# add a new key value pair\n","my_dict[\"Bike\"] = \"Yamaha\"\n","print(\"After adding new key:\", my_dict)\n","\n","\n","# Now, add many elements of your choice to this dictionary. do it in one line\n","my_dict.update({\"Name\": \"Risha\", \"Age\": 18, \"City\": \"Mumbai\"})\n","print(\"After adding many elements:\", my_dict)\n","# Try removing element at index 2\n","\n","key_to_remove = list(my_dict.keys())[2]\n","\n","removed_value = my_dict.pop(key_to_remove)\n","print(f\"Removed element at index 2: {key_to_remove} -> {removed_value}\")\n","print(\"Dictionary after removal:\", my_dict)\n","\n","\n","# Now, return the element with the key 'Bike'\n","bike_value = my_dict[\"Bike\"]\n","print(\"Value of ke